\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Does Participation in Extracurricular Activities Improve Performance in
 School?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 26, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the most recent recession, there have been decreases to public funding
 across the board.
 Even though education is the last budget areas to be cut, many districts
 are dealing with declines in both state and local funding, as well as rising
 costs.
 Despite the recession ending in 2009, many districts will be unable to
 regain their pre-2008 fudning until several years from now.
 Consequently, school districts are forced to make difficult decisions in
 order to balance their budgets while still meeting the needs of students.
 Since extracurricular activities are considered 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

non-essential,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 they have faced some of the deepest budgetary cuts and restrictions.
 However, many opponents of these cuts claim that reducing or eliminating
 extracurrilar activities does more harm to the students than school administrat
ors anticipate.
 In fact, there is a strong correlation between involvement in extracurricular
 activities and positive performance in school.
 Yet, as we know, correlation does not imply causation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With my project, I intend to measure the effect participating in extracurricular
 activities has on student grades.
 The notable problem facing this examination is the fact that the treatment
 variable is endogenous.
 We cannot rule out the fact that participating in extracurricular activities
 may be correlated with some omitted variable, such as innate ability, which
 also affects grades.
 To solve this problem, I will employ an instrumental variable to isolate
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

as-good-as-random
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation in the treatment variable.
 The instrumental variable I propose is whether or not a student's parents
 were also involved in extracurricular activities while they were in school.
 This instrumental variable is correlated with the endogenous variable because
 a student is more likely to join a particular extracurricular activity
 if a parent was also involved in said activity.
 Yet, the instrument is uncorrelated with the outcome because parents' participa
tion in an extracurricular activity while they were in school has absolutely
 no affect on their child's current performance in school.
 The only way this instrument can have an effect on the outcome is through
 the treatment variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This will be a panel data analysis where each cross-section observation
 represents a student.
 I will measure the effect participating in an extracurricular activity
 has on a student's grades over time.
 I should be able to obtain data directly from schools as long as the students
 are kept anonymous, so as not to violate FERPA.
\end_layout

